# Experiment 4: Storage is a plain store — where it lives per platform (nsh 0.4.0 e626a8d)
Date: 2026-11-07 (UTC)

## What was read
1. Generated TSX types: .nsh/types/nsh/index.d.ts (scratch app, generated by nsh 0.4.0):

   /** The app's key-value storage: strings by string key, kept on the device
    * across launches (up to 5 MB). Calls run in order, so a read after a write
    * sees it. */
   export interface StorageModule {
     getItem(key: string): Promise<string | null>;
     setItem(key: string, value: string): Promise<void>;  // ERR_NSH_QUOTA_EXCEEDED
     removeItem(key: string): Promise<void>;
     keys(): Promise<(string)[]>;
     clear(): Promise<void>;
   }
   export declare const Storage: StorageModule;

   No encryption, no access control, no per-item class. Plain strings.

2. Rust source (installed crate): ~/.cargo/registry/src/index.crates.io-*/nsh-core-0.4.0/src/storage.rs
   - "kept on the device by the platform's StorageBackend (a file in the app's
     data directory, the browser's localStorage)". QUOTA = 5 MiB.
   - The core keeps entries in memory over a `StorageBackend` trait
     (load()/write()); nothing cryptographic anywhere in the module.

3. File backend: /Users/hmziq/fo/.refs/nsh-v0.4.0/crates/nsh-engine/src/storage.rs
   - "The app's storage in a file (nsh/storage on the native platforms): its
     entries as one JSON object, rewritten whole (to a temporary file, then
     renamed over it) ... FileStorage { path } ... `dir/storage.json`".
   - data_dir() per platform (same file, lines 64-87):
       "macos" | "ios"  -> $HOME/Library/Application Support
       "windows"        -> %APPDATA%
       others (linux)   -> $XDG_DATA_HOME, else $HOME/.local/share
       "android"        -> None (only the platform knows its files directory)
   - The app's own dir is <data_dir>/<app id>, so the file is e.g.
     ~/Library/Application Support/<app-id>/storage.json — one plain JSON
     object, world-readable to anything that can read that user's files.

4. Web backend: /Users/hmziq/fo/.refs/nsh-v0.4.0/crates/nsh-platform-web/src/storage.rs
   - "The app's storage (nsh/storage) in the browser's localStorage ... under
     the app's prefix (its id, else its page's path)". localStorage is exactly
     what OWASP says must not hold tokens ("Do not store session identifiers in
     local storage as the data is always accessible by JavaScript").

## Conclusion (what each surface needs)
- Storage (Rust `nsh_core::storage::Storage` / TSX `Storage`) is for drafts,
  flags and UI state. Refresh tokens and access tokens NEVER go in it; the
  storage proof test must grep storage.json and logs for tokens.
- iOS/macOS: Keychain (kSecClassGenericPassword, ThisDeviceOnly for refresh
  tokens) — reachable from native code of the Rust crate or the embedding host;
  ASIdentity… n/a. Android: Android Keystore directly (androidx.security-crypto's
  EncryptedSharedPreferences is deprecated as of security-crypto 1.1.0-beta01,
  June 2025: "Deprecated all APIs in favour of existing platform APIs and direct
  use of Android Keystore"). Windows: DPAPI CryptProtectData (user scope) over a
  file the app owns. Linux: Secret Service (org.freedesktop.secrets; gnome-keyring
  or KWallet), with an encrypted-file fallback when no daemon. Web: no token in
  localStorage/sessionStorage per OWASP; keep tokens server-side or in an
  HttpOnly cookie of a backend, or accept an explicitly named weaker mode.
